KUtrace for NVIDIA Grace (GB200/GB300), Ubuntu linux-nvidia 7.0, arm64

Base tree: https://github.com/NVIDIA/NV-Kernels branch 24.04_linux-nvidia-7.0-next
  commit 2234cd7f8f8a9fdbceef37f82c531016aab8cf73
  (Ubuntu-nvidia-7.0-7.0.0-1022.23~24.04.1, Linux 7.0.14)
Flavour: Ubuntu nvidia-64k (config from debian.nvidia-7.0/config/annotations)

STATUS: cross-compiled only (clang, LLVM=1, nvidia-64k config + CONFIG_KUTRACE=y).
Not yet booted or traced on Grace hardware.

(1) Patch and build
$ git clone --depth 1 -b 24.04_linux-nvidia-7.0-next https://github.com/NVIDIA/NV-Kernels
$ cd NV-Kernels
$ git apply ../kutrace_patch_arm64_linux-nvidia-7.0.patch
$ scripts/config --enable KUTRACE
  then build and install as usual for your distro.

(2) Module: build ../module with KDIR pointing at this tree
$ make -C ../module KDIR=/path/to/NV-Kernels
$ sudo insmod kutrace_mod.ko tracemb=<MB>   [llc_event=0x17 if LL_CACHE_MISS_RD reads zero]

(3) Postproc: build ../../postproc natively on the Grace node. Regenerate the
device-IRQ names for that node with
$ postproc/gen_irqnames_from_proc.py postproc/kutrace_control_names_linux_grace.h

Notes
 - Run kutrace_control on the host, not inside a container: the default
   seccomp profile blocks syscall 1023.
 - Device IRQs are recorded as Linux virq % 236; 236 is the arch timer,
   246..253 are the arm64 IPIs, 254 is the WFI idle hint.
 - The time base is CNTVCT >> 4 (1 GHz -> 62.5 MHz); kutrace_lib.cc applies
   the same shift in user space.
